# QA Prompts

In this notebook you tune the instruction and the demonstrations of a small
question-answering predictor in one search. `:mipro_v2` asks a proposal model
for new instructions, asks a teacher model for demonstrations, and searches
their combinations on training minibatches. Only full validation results can
win. The notebook runs offline with no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.20"}
])
```

## Script the models

`Scripted` serves OpenAI-compatible replies through an in-process Plug, so
ReqLLM runs its real request path without a network call. Each role gets its
own reply function of the decoded request body. In real use you pass a real
model spec and the host's credentials for each role instead.

```elixir
defmodule Scripted do
  @moduledoc "Serves scripted OpenAI-compatible replies to ReqLLM through an in-process Plug."

  def model, do: %{provider: :openai, id: "gpt-4-turbo", extra: %{wire: %{protocol: "openai_chat"}}}

  def options(reply) when is_function(reply, 1) do
    plug = fn conn ->
      {:ok, body, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
      respond(conn, reply.(JSON.decode!(body)))
    end

    [api_key: "scripted", req_http_options: [plug: plug], max_retries: 0]
  end

  def role(reply), do: [model: model(), adapter: Tune.Adapter.Chat, request_options: options(reply)]

  def chat(fields) do
    Enum.map_join(fields, "\n\n", fn {name, value} ->
      text = if is_binary(value), do: value, else: JSON.encode!(value)
      "[[ ## #{name} ## ]]\n#{text}"
    end) <> "\n\n[[ ## completed ## ]]"
  end

  def system(body), do: hd(body["messages"])["content"]

  def field(body, name) do
    text = body["messages"] |> Enum.filter(&(&1["role"] == "user")) |> List.last() |> Map.fetch!("content")

    case Regex.run(~r/\[\[ ## #{name} ## \]\]\n(.*?)(?=\n\n\[\[ ## |\n\nRespond with|\z)/s, text) do
      [_, value] -> value
      nil -> nil
    end
  end

  def demos?(body), do: Enum.any?(body["messages"], &(&1["role"] == "assistant"))

  defp respond(conn, text) do
    send_json(conn, 200, %{
      id: "scripted",
      model: "gpt-4-turbo",
      choices: [%{message: %{role: "assistant", content: text}, finish_reason: "stop"}],
      usage: %{prompt_tokens: 10, completion_tokens: 5, total_tokens: 15}
    })
  end

  defp send_json(conn, status, body) do
    conn
    |> Plug.Conn.put_resp_content_type("application/json")
    |> Plug.Conn.send_resp(status, JSON.encode!(body))
  end
end
```

## Define the predictor and data

Each case gives a short context and a question. The score is 1.0 for the
exact expected answer.

```elixir
{:ok, qa} =
  Tune.Predictor.new(
    signature: [
      instructions: "Answer the question.",
      inputs: [context: Zoi.string(), question: Zoi.string()],
      outputs: [answer: Zoi.string()]
    ]
  )

rows = fn items ->
  Enum.map(items, fn {id, question, subject, answer} ->
    %{
      id: id,
      input: %{context: "About #{subject}: #{answer}. An unrelated note.", question: question},
      expected: %{answer: answer}
    }
  end)
end

train =
  rows.([
    {"tower", "Which city is home to the Eiffel Tower?", "the Eiffel Tower", "Paris"},
    {"novel", "Who wrote Pride and Prejudice?", "Pride and Prejudice", "Jane Austen"},
    {"plants", "Which gas do plants absorb?", "plants", "carbon dioxide"},
    {"planet", "Which planet is called the red planet?", "the red planet", "Mars"}
  ])

validation =
  rows.([
    {"ocean", "What is the largest ocean?", "oceans", "Pacific"},
    {"metal", "Which metal is liquid at room temperature?", "metals", "mercury"},
    {"river", "Which river flows through Cairo?", "Cairo", "the Nile"}
  ])

test =
  rows.([
    {"bridge", "Which city has the Golden Gate Bridge?", "the Golden Gate Bridge", "San Francisco"},
    {"painter", "Who painted the Mona Lisa?", "the Mona Lisa", "Leonardo da Vinci"}
  ])

:ok
```

## Configure three roles

Target, teacher, and proposal are separate model configurations.

- The target answers in a full sentence by default. Under an instruction
  containing "Memorize", it recalls training answers and knows nothing else.
  Under an instruction containing "Quote" with at least one demonstration,
  it quotes the answer from the context.
- The teacher quotes the answer from the context. Its replies become
  bootstrapped demonstrations.
- The proposal model returns two new instructions.

```elixir
remembered = Map.new(train, &{&1.input.question, &1.expected.answer})

quote_answer = fn body ->
  [_, answer] = Regex.run(~r/: (.*?)\./, Scripted.field(body, "context"))
  answer
end

target = fn body ->
  instruction = Scripted.system(body)

  answer =
    cond do
      instruction =~ "Memorize" -> Map.get(remembered, Scripted.field(body, "question"), "unknown")
      instruction =~ "Quote" and Scripted.demos?(body) -> quote_answer.(body)
      true -> "I believe the answer is #{quote_answer.(body)}."
    end

  Scripted.chat(answer: answer)
end

teacher = fn body -> Scripted.chat(answer: quote_answer.(body)) end

proposal = fn _body ->
  Scripted.chat(
    instructions: ["Memorize the answers to past questions.", "Quote the shortest answer from the context."]
  )
end

:ok
```

## Run the search

`num_candidates: 3` gives three instructions (the original plus two
proposals) and three demonstration sets (none plus two bootstrapped sets).
The seed fixes minibatch sampling and the sampler, and `model_calls: 60`
caps target, teacher, and proposal calls together.

```elixir
study =
  Tune.new(qa, model: Scripted.role(target), teacher: Scripted.role(teacher), proposal: Scripted.role(proposal))
  |> Tune.data(train: train, validation: validation, test: test)
  |> Tune.score(:answer, fn row, output ->
    if output["answer"] == row.expected["answer"], do: 1.0, else: 0.0
  end)
  |> Tune.search(:mipro_v2,
    accept_score: 1.0,
    num_candidates: 3,
    num_trials: 10,
    minibatch_size: 2,
    minibatch_full_eval_steps: 3,
    n_startup: 3,
    max_labeled_demos: 0,
    max_bootstrapped_demos: 1
  )
  |> Tune.limit(seed: 5, model_calls: 60)

{:ok, winner} = Tune.tune(study)

IO.inspect(winner.calls, label: "calls")
IO.inspect(winner.stop_reason, label: "stop")
```

```text
calls: %{target: 35, teacher: 2, proposal: 1}
stop: :completed
```

## Find the overfit candidate

`winner.metadata.notes.instructions` lists the instruction choices by index.
Each trial scores one choice on a two-case training minibatch.

```elixir
instructions = winner.metadata.notes.instructions["root"]
Enum.with_index(instructions, fn text, index -> IO.puts("#{index}: #{text}") end)

for trial <- winner.metadata.notes.trials do
  IO.puts(
    "trial #{trial.trial}: instruction #{trial.choice["root.instruction"]}, " <>
      "demos #{trial.choice["root.demos"]}, minibatch #{trial.score}"
  )
end

:ok
```

```text
0: Answer the question.
1: Memorize the answers to past questions.
2: Quote the shortest answer from the context.
trial 1: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
trial 2: instruction 2, demos 2, minibatch 1.0
trial 3: instruction 1, demos 0, minibatch 1.0
trial 4: instruction 0, demos 1, minibatch 0.0
trial 5: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
trial 6: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
trial 7: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
trial 8: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
trial 9: instruction 0, demos 0, minibatch 0.0
trial 10: instruction 1, demos 1, minibatch 1.0
```

The memorizing instruction scores 1.0 on every training minibatch. Every
`minibatch_full_eval_steps` trials, the best unpromoted choice gets a full
validation pass. `winner.history` holds those validated candidates.

```elixir
for candidate <- winner.history do
  IO.puts(
    "#{candidate.metadata.label} #{inspect(candidate.metadata[:choice])} " <>
      "validation #{candidate.evaluation.selected_score}"
  )
end

:ok
```

```text
baseline nil validation 0.0
trial %{"root.demos" => 0, "root.instruction" => 1} validation 0.0
trial %{"root.demos" => 1, "root.instruction" => 1} validation 0.0
trial %{"root.demos" => 2, "root.instruction" => 2} validation 1.0
trial %{"root.demos" => 1, "root.instruction" => 0} validation 0.0
```

Both memorizing candidates looked perfect on training minibatches and score
0.0 on validation, because the validation questions were never in training.
Minibatch scores only guide the sampler; they never become the result.

## Inspect the winner

`winner.value` is the tuned predictor. It keeps the quoting instruction and
one bootstrapped demonstration from the teacher.

```elixir
IO.puts(winner.value.signature.instructions)

for demo <- winner.value.demos do
  IO.puts("#{demo.inputs["question"]} -> #{demo.outputs["answer"]}")
end
```

```text
Quote the shortest answer from the context.
Which gas do plants absorb? -> carbon dioxide
```

## Compare on the test cases

```elixir
{:ok, comparison} = Tune.compare(study, winner, split: :test)

for id <- comparison.case_ids do
  row = comparison.rows[id]
  IO.puts("#{id}: #{inspect(row.baseline.output["answer"])} -> #{inspect(row.challenger.output["answer"])}")
end

Enum.sort(comparison.metrics.answer)
```

```text
bridge: "I believe the answer is San Francisco." -> "San Francisco"
painter: "I believe the answer is Leonardo da Vinci." -> "Leonardo da Vinci"
```

```text
[
  baseline_mean: 0.0,
  challenger_mean: 1.0,
  counts: %{improved: 2},
  delta: 1.0,
  direction: :maximize,
  paired_count: 2,
  status: :improved
]
```

A smaller `model_calls` limit stops the search early. The result then has
`complete?: false` and `stop_reason: :budget_exhausted`, and holds the best
fully validated candidate so far. With `model_calls: 25` here, that is still
the baseline instruction.

